==Old English==

===Etymology===
From Germanic ''*halðan'' ‘to watch, look after’.  Cognate with Old Frisian ''[[halda]]'', Old Saxon ''[[haldan]]'' (Dutch ''[[houden]]''), Old High German ''[[haltan]]'' (German ''[[halten]]''), Old Norse ''[[halda]]'' (Swedish ''[[hålla]]''), Gothic '''[[𐌷𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽]]'''.

===Pronunciation===
{{IPA|/ˈhæɑldɑn/}}

===Verb===
'''healdan''' (class VII strong: ''third-person sg present'' '''[[hielt]]''', ''third-person sg preterite'' {{Latinx|'''[[heold|hēold]]'''}}, ''preterite plural'' {{Latinx|'''[[heoldon|hēoldon]]'''}}, ''past participle'' ('''ġe''')'''[[healden]]''')

# to keep watch over (cattle etc.)
# to [[hold]] fast, to [[grasp]]
# to [[contain]]
# to [[possess]]
# {{intransitive|lang=ang}} to maintain one’s position against an enemy

===Descendants===
*English: [[hold]]

